  <HOMEWORK title="Bring-Up 9: Zero errors isn't the same as validated" prompt="Every earlier step is fixed and correctly brought up. Run the final validation the way a real bring-up lab would, and decide: is this link actually ready to ship?"
            hint="Zero errors over a short run doesn't, by itself, tell you the BER. What's the actual confidence bound on the number you just measured, and how does it compare to a real link's typical spec (often 1e-12 or tighter)?"
            explanation="Everything here is correctly brought up and a short run comes back with zero bit errors out of roughly 66,000 compared - which sounds perfect, but zero errors only gives you an upper bound on the true BER, not the true BER itself. By the &quot;rule of three&quot; (3 divided by the bit count), that upper bound is only about 4.5e-5 at 95% confidence here. Real SerDes specs are routinely 1e-12 or tighter - proving that statistically would take on the order of a trillion compared bits, not sixty-six thousand. The honest conclusion from this run is &quot;no errors seen yet, with a lot of margin left to prove,&quot; not &quot;validated to spec.&quot; Deciding how long a real bring-up run actually needs to go - and watching eye margin as a leading indicator along the way, the way Bring-Up 6 did - is the real last step of a validation plan, not just watching the error counter stay at zero for a while."/>
